  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAn initial band of showers crosses the area today as a front \napproaches from the west, moving into New Hampshire between 8 \nand 9 AM and reaching the Maine boarder before noon. These \nshowers looks to move out before sunset and ultimately won't \namount to much QPF-wise. Amounts in New Hampshire look to range \nfrom a tenth to a quarter with the Seacoast having a better \nchance of only seeing a few hundredths. The frontal boundary \nslows and stalls Thursday night with shower activity continuing \nalong it. Models have come to a consensus that it stalls at the \ncoast or just offshore and will become a focus for an axis of \nheavier rain as it taps into tropical moisture and drives PWATs \nupwards of 1.25 to 1.40, well above normal for this time of \nyear. However, models show dry air intruding on the back side of\nthe front so the precipitation shield could be pretty sharp as \nlow pressure rides along the front through Friday. This would \nresult in a soaking rainfall confined to the coast with not too \nmuch occuring north of the foothills. All hope is not lost for \nthese locations though as models are also suggesting a localized\ncoastal low spinning up on Saturday that would help to push \nrain further inland, but it would not be at the magnitude the \ncoast sees the day before. As models trend the axis of heavier \nrain further off shore NBM probabilities of greater than an inch\nof rain in the Friday through Saturday timeframe have come down\nquite a bit since this time last night. The coast is now in the\n40 to 50 percent range with slightly higher probabilities for \nthe Midcoast. If we drop this down to a half inch we get \nprobabilities 50 to 60 percent from the foothills down to the \ncoast (with closer to 70 percent probabilities at the coast). \nWith 30 to 40 percent probabilities in northern New Hampshire \nand the Western Maine Mountains. I hate to say it, but it \ncertainly looks like we are trending toward the rug getting \npulled again, but at this point any rain is beneficial as many \nplaces have not seen measurable rain in over two weeks. \n\nTroughing persists into the middle of next week, but at this \ntime it looks like it is only going to be good for upslope rain \nand snow showers in the mountains with locations south of the \nmountains returning to mostly dry weather.",
